}^{2}$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$52x$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$39$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$40x$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$39$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore, we can write ${x}^{4}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4} & =q(x)p(x)+(-40x-39),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{2}-4x+13.$  Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{4}=q(A)p(A)+(-40A-39I).\n$$\n\nNow, according to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & =q(A)p(A)+(-40A-39I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(-40A-39I) \\\\  & =-40A-39I.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
